787" :question/id #uuid "dcc2043b-54b0-52ae-b83e-ebaa5b4ad306" :question/math-academy-id "q-41787" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/7a3c82568cfaf845708a9dcee79a3ac2e0e9d2131776fd1c7daa1c598473f3b3.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=10$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(10,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5\\sqrt{3},-5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-41787/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41787"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-87135/selection/7e2b48383d5d1cc80a4b40001f958af2e676fec2cb1400c3d40af53d8105a997" :answer/id #uuid "dc805390-9abf-5f6f-8121-b7c1ae42e7e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87135/selection/004b40e9dbf8f036600a30e235f0e875769fd38731126675bdaffac624ba888c" :answer/id #uuid "bec8f012-4ed2-52de-b3a0-7b7f50c94af7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87135/selection/7d6a9e926adb7325d8a9ca1b402a6d19db0b8e2b59c1f1080d875510213f6120" :answer/id #uuid "84688193-ce7d-51c2-ac01-e8d152389ee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87135/selection/92ed4dc763639740991b294b2e716df07a62a010be23a205b4128998a6035ea6" :answer/id #uuid "2dc6991b-0ac3-5fda-bc50-185160b7f02e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87135/selection/9d4d1c66e82dbe006fc485d871e23727a78e849470fa0546d1e250098f1ba0f7" :answer/id #uuid "107d6ff3-45d7-5b64-a584-89049c5e7a7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87135/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "80659fd2-55b4-5467-bd0a-018342344258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-87135/selection/004b40e9dbf8f036600a30e235f0e875769fd38731126675bdaffac624ba888c" :answer-field/choices ["q-87135/selection/7e2b48383d5d1cc80a4b40001f958af2e676fec2cb1400c3d40af53d8105a997" "q-87135/selection/004b40e9dbf8f036600a30e235f0e875769fd38731126675bdaffac624ba888c" "q-87135/selection/7d6a9e926adb7325d8a9ca1b402a6d19db0b8e2b59c1f1080d875510213f6120" "q-87135/selection/92ed4dc763639740991b294b2e716df07a62a010be23a205b4128998a6035ea6" "q-87135/selection/9d4d1c66e82dbe006fc485d871e23727a78e849470fa0546d1e250098f1ba0f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-87135" :question/id #uuid "6b7e4f86-ed4a-5213-ac2e-f26cf9fc765f" :question/math-academy-id "q-87135" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/5507850932bb4061cd19cdbffa18aacf2b235293e643170ee78c4326a96e050d.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(9,-3\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{9}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-87135/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-87135"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120712/selection/e8333d521331a537ffa1342cb69c44a3fc695d9336fd3c013730049880b2aca3" :answer/id #uuid "005ae058-7214-5a7b-aba6-49fed4a0328d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120712/selection/bbe61327a3d2cab56dd8b8c1d4257f7fd1b6458c13f98ae1ba685f40387606ae" :answer/id #uuid "3942dabf-b913-5840-8d0d-9b399dc4ec6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120712/selection/6bd4cd936f7536fedfb8fb7d36c0f6998e178a23f1310130f16245da0f55ec32" :answer/id #uuid "c0601487-2cc9-5ae6-aba6-ab2ef75badd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120712/selection/e11120e62f1aa183245e121f00e47ebd77ea4b6960481d9b3f83755d0f8ce826" :answer/id #uuid "f9c4c1b0-f47d-5750-8b1e-2ed53ab0587b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120712/selection/fc1037cd53776d4d6209e4d3a058826aece3f4c4b821d8b2e191e9ccb621064f" :answer/id #uuid "62994270-e8cc-504d-b12a-6812b6b6ca22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\fr