0719/selection/f408214744090bee769b82d44541da55896e1f06cf369a96769bd9fab4263c45" "q-120719/selection/d8ef6e905889e15d3572b9474abcdb0273187088f42bfea2b86e1911e03ad1e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120719" :question/id #uuid "9300a06e-224a-5760-bf8d-1bf574e7c275" :question/math-academy-id "q-120719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120719/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-321322/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer/id #uuid "b5301351-f3bd-52a5-947f-cca1cb8e05a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321322/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a844af0-2205-5ab8-9808-063dc88aee5a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321322/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer-field/choices ["q-321322/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321322" :question/id #uuid "f9a133c1-2916-570f-b68c-988d4cc04c8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-321322" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/c922feb998c41aae1ab40e3e6f240b07756699d4756b91458ff8271cec152f7f.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the ray shown above. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nThe polar equation is $\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-321322/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321322"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
